\subsection{Noise-Free Model-Mismatch Floor}
\label{sec:model-mismatch-ablation}

A separate ablation removes simulated sensor corruption while leaving each
estimator otherwise in its deployed configuration.  The eight stationary
JONSWAP and PM--Stokes records are replayed through OU--II, OU--III, and TFG
with the simulator's \texttt{--no-noise} option.  Accelerometer and gyroscope
white noise, initial bias, bias random walk, and magnetometer noise and
calibration perturbations are therefore absent, while the physical wave and
attitude motion are unchanged.  The estimators are \emph{not} retuned for ideal
measurements: their process/measurement covariances, pseudo-measurements,
adaptation, startup logic, and regularization remain those used in normal
operation.  The experiment consequently measures a deployed
estimator/model-mismatch floor, not pure plant-model mismatch.

All values in Table~\ref{tab:model-mismatch-floor} pool the final \SI{900}{s}
of the eight equal-length records.  With identical window lengths, the pooled
RMS is the RMS obtained by concatenating the eight scored windows.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Noise-free deployed-estimator mismatch floor over eight stationary
  records.  $Z/Z_{\rm ref}$ is normalized by pooled reference vertical RMS.}
  \label{tab:model-mismatch-floor}
  \footnotesize
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.4pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
    \toprule
    Family & $X$ [m] & $Y$ [m] & $Z$ [m] & 3-D [m] & $Z/Z_{\rm ref}$ [\%] \\
    \midrule
    OU--II  & 0.333 & 0.224 & 0.240 & 0.467 & 20.32 \\
    OU--III & 0.246 & 0.174 & 0.140 & 0.332 & 11.87 \\
    TFG     & 0.542 & 0.199 & 0.155 & 0.598 & 13.15 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

OU--III has the lowest pooled displacement floor: vertical RMS is
\SI{0.140}{m} and 3-D RMS is \SI{0.332}{m}, compared with
\SI{0.240}{m} and \SI{0.467}{m} for OU--II.  TFG is close to OU--III
vertically at \SI{0.155}{m}, but its \SI{0.542}{m} $X$-axis floor drives the
3-D value to \SI{0.598}{m}.  The residual therefore does not vanish when the
sensor corruption is removed.  Across individual records, vertical residual
error remains approximately \pct{2.9}--\pct{3.4} of $H_s$ for OU--III,
\pct{5.0}--\pct{5.45} for OU--II, and \pct{2.94}--\pct{3.35} for TFG, which is
consistent with a substantial deterministic estimator/regularization component.

The zero-bias input also exposes estimator-generated bias states.  Pooled
accelerometer-bias-state RMS is \SI{0.0132}{m.s^{-2}} for OU--III,
\SI{0.0384}{m.s^{-2}} for OU--II, and \SI{0.0254}{m.s^{-2}} for TFG.  These are
not physical sensor-bias errors in this experiment because the injected true
bias is zero; they quantify bias state used by each estimator while fitting
ideal but model-mismatched motion.

The result should not be attributed to the OU or TFG stochastic model alone.
The virtual measurements intentionally distort low-frequency physical motion,
the adaptive operating point has finite bandwidth, attitude error can feed
gravity into horizontal acceleration, and the filters retain measurement
covariances calibrated for real sensors.  The ablation therefore bounds the
combined residual of these deployed estimator choices under ideal input
measurements rather than isolating a single source of model error.
